5. Array Methods
Array methods are very important in modern JavaScript and especially important when learning React JS.
Consider:
const numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50];

forEach()
Used to execute a function for every element.
numbers.forEach(number => {
    console.log(number);
});
Output:
10
20
30
40
50

map()
Used to create a new array by transforming each element.
const numbers = [10, 20, 30];

const result = numbers.map(number => number * 2);

console.log(result);
Output:
[20, 40, 60]
Example
const names = ["rahul", "priya", "amit"];

const upperNames = names.map(name => name.toUpperCase());

console.log(upperNames);
Output:
["RAHUL", "PRIYA", "AMIT"]

filter()
Used to select elements based on a condition.
const numbers = [10, 15, 20, 25, 30];

const result = numbers.filter(number => number > 20);

console.log(result);
Output:
[25, 30]

find()
Returns the first element that matches the condition.
const numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40];

const result = numbers.find(number => number > 20);

console.log(result);
Output:
30

some()
Checks whether at least one element satisfies the condition.
const numbers = [10, 20, 30];

const result = numbers.some(number => number > 25);

console.log(result);
Output:
true

every()
Checks whether all elements satisfy the condition.
const numbers = [10, 20, 30];

const result = numbers.every(number => number > 5);

console.log(result);
Output:
true

includes()
Checks whether an array contains a particular value.
const fruits = ["Apple", "Mango", "Banana"];

console.log(fruits.includes("Mango"));
Output:
true

